# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scene 1: Monday Afternoon - The Airlock
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Narrator: [Scene 1: Monday Afternoon - The Airlock] [HIDE: all]
Narrator: [BG: Biotech_Lab.jpg] [BACKGROUND: The shared Molecular Biotechnology laboratory (Biotech 200), Monday, 4:30 p.m. Pristine black epoxy counters, the cool violet-white glow of laminar flow hoods, and neatly racked rows of Eppendorf tubes on crushed ice. Late afternoon autumn sunlight filters through the venetian blinds, casting striped shadows across the sterile linoleum.]
Narrator: [AUDIO: The rhythmic, high-pitched hum of a refrigerated microcentrifuge in the corner, layered with the sharp, satisfying *click-clack* of a P2 micropipette.]
Narrator: [SPRITE: Nora_Calm, middle] The Gilson micropipette -- which Nora has privately christened 'Sir Clicks-a-Lot' with a label maker -- clicks against Nora's gloved thumb with a small, crisp, mechanical chime.
Narrator: Two microliters of CRISPR-Cas12a guide RNA, drawn up clean into the capillary tip. Zero air bubbles. Zero meniscus drift.
Narrator: She loves this part. She has loved it since her very first molecular techniques lab freshman year. It is the one hour of the day where the entire chaotic, demanding universe narrows down to a forty-microliter reaction volume, a steady hand, a sixty-second breath, and a set of biochemical laws that never lie to you.
Nora_Calm(middle): (V.O.) Enzymes don't guilt-trip you. Buffer solutions don't borrow your favorite sweater, spill salsa on the sleeve, and claim it had that stain when they took it. If you respect the stoichiometry, the chemistry respects you back.
Dr. Okafor: You make that look like breathing, Nora.
Narrator: Nora doesn't jerk or look up until she has smoothly dispensed the droplet against the inner wall of the PCR tube and withdrawn the tip past the first stop. Only then does she eject the plastic tip into the sharps bin and glance over her shoulder with a quiet smile.
Narrator: Six months into the rapid sepsis biosensor project, and her hands have stopped needing her conscious brain's permission to execute the protocol. The electrochemical CRISPR diagnostic she helped engineer can flag a lethal gram-negative bloodstream infection from whole blood in forty-two minutes instead of the forty-eight hours a traditional hospital blood culture takes.
Narrator: On Thursday afternoon at 2:30 p.m., she will stand on the main stage of the Undergraduate Research Symposium and present it as one of five university-wide finalists for the Callahan Prize.
Nora_Smiling(middle): It kind of is like breathing by now, Dr. Okafor. Just muscle memory, RNaseZap, and an irresponsible amount of dark roast.
Dr. Okafor: (leaning against the adjacent bench, arms crossed over her white lab coat with quiet, maternal pride) Don't sell yourself short with the caffeine jokes when the judges come around on Thursday. Half the postdocs in this building couldn't have salvaged the thiol-gold electrode binding after Prototype Eleven failed last month. You're the one who figured out the spacer length was causing steric hindrance.
Nora_Speaking(middle): Prototype Eleven was a spectacular disaster. I still have the dead cyclic voltammetry curves framed on my Graveyard Tab in Excel.
Dr. Okafor: And Prototype Twelve hit 99.4% sensitivity in spiked serum this morning. Have your parents or Camille confirmed what time they're getting into town on Thursday? I can have the department administrator reserve two guest lanyards at the CID Atrium check-in desk.
Narrator: [SPRITE: Nora_Tired, middle] A tiny, invisible muscle below Nora's left ear tightens. She keeps her smile politely fixed in place so Dr. Okafor won't see the flicker.
Nora_Speaking(middle): Um -- don't worry about the guest lanyards yet, Dr. Okafor. My brother Daniel has a second-round analyst interview with a finance firm in the city this week, so my mom and dad's schedule is kind of up in the air. And Camille's still trying to see if she can Zoom into the livestream between her thesis committee meetings.
Dr. Okafor: (frowning faintly, studying Nora's face for a beat too long) Nora. The Callahan Prize is the highest undergraduate research distinction in the college of sciences. You've been working sixty-hour weeks on this assay since March.
Nora_Calm(middle): They know, Dr. Okafor. Really. My family's just not super fluent in molecular biology. They're proud in their own way.
Narrator: Dr. Okafor looks like she wants to say something else, thinks better of overstepping, and pats the edge of Nora's bench gently before walking back toward her office.
Narrator: Almost on cue, before the office door even clicks shut, Nora's phone buzzes violently against the black epoxy countertop. Once. Twice. Three times in five seconds.
Narrator: The screen lights up with a contact photo taken two summers ago at the beach: *Camille <3*.
Narrator: *Camille: ugh my roommate literally did the thing again. the sink thing.*\n*Camille: she left three days of crusty pasta bowls right on top of my strainer when she KNOWS my thesis prospectus is due Friday and I can't function in a cluttered space.*\n*Camille: I can't even talk about it I'm shaking I'm so mad. I swear to god she's doing it just to sabotage my headspace. are you free??*
Narrator: Nora strips off her nitrile gloves and looks down at the glowing glass. Then she looks at her ice bucket. Her master mix of Cas12a ribonucleoprotein complexes is sitting at 4°C; if she doesn't load the 96-well plate into the fluorometer for her final symposium validation run within the next fifteen minutes, the enzyme activity will start to drift.
Narrator: Yet she knows every beat of this choreography by heart. If she doesn't answer immediately, the crisis about the pasta bowls will mutate into a crisis about how Nora is "emotionally distant."

? Narrator: How does Nora handle Camille's familiar spiral while her time-sensitive RNA sits on ice?
    > "That sounds so frustrating. Did you say something to her?" (Engage and absorb the emotional labor)
        Narrator: [SPRITE: Nora_Tired, middle] The old peacemaker reflex wins. Nora leans against the counter, picks up the phone with her ungloved hand, and types back quickly to keep the peace.\n*Nora: That sounds so frustrating, Cam. I'm sorry she keeps doing that. Did you say something to her?*
        Narrator: The three typing bubbles appear in less than two seconds. Camille launches into a massive, six-paragraph wall of text dissecting every passive-aggressive sticky note her roommate has written since September.
        Narrator: Nora sighs -- a quiet, sixty-second metronome exhale that fogs the edge of her safety glasses -- and stands there under the humming UV hood, validating Camille's anger, offering gentle reframes, and talking her down from the ledge while the crushed ice around her PCR tubes slowly melts into slush.
        Narrator: Finally, after twelve minutes of playing therapist, Nora types a message of her own:\n*Nora: Glad you're feeling a little calmer. Pretty huge day in the lab here, actually -- Prototype 12 hit 99.4% sensitivity and Dr. Okafor just confirmed my 2:30 run-through slot for Thursday's Callahan finals!*
        Narrator: The three typing bubbles pop up, flicker for a second, and vanish. A *Read 4:44 PM* receipt appears underneath Nora's message. Camille doesn't reply. Now that her own crisis has been vented, she has already put her phone down.
        Narrator: Nora stares at the single gray bubble of her own good news sitting unanswered at the bottom of the screen, feeling a quiet, familiar hollowness ache behind her ribs.
        System: [STAT_UPDATE: +Emotional Labor, -Energy]
    > "I'm sorry, Cam. I'm deep in CRISPR assays right now. Can we talk tonight?" (Set a gentle boundary)
        Narrator: [SPRITE: Nora_Calm, middle] Nora stares at the glowing paragraphs about pasta bowls. Then she looks at the tiny, clear droplets of guide RNA sitting in her ice block -- six months of her own life, four days away from the biggest presentation of her college career.
        Narrator: Feeling the familiar gravitational pull of Camille's daily emergency trying to drag her out of her airlock, Nora takes a steady breath and types a firm, gentle boundary:\n*Nora: I'm really sorry she's stressing you out, Cam. I'm elbow-deep in time-sensitive CRISPR assays right now for my Thursday symposium finals and can't text for the next hour. Can I call you tonight at nine when I'm home?*
        Narrator: Forty seconds pass. The screen flashes with a single reply:\n*Camille: wow okay. glad to know your test tubes are more important than your gf having a breakdown. sorry to bother you with my life.*
        Narrator: [SPRITE: Nora_Distressed, middle] The guilt hits Nora's stomach like a shot of cold acid. Her thumb hovers over the keyboard, itching to type an immediate three-paragraph apology and abandon her bench.
        Narrator: Instead, she swallows the lump in her throat, clicks the side button to darken the screen, and places the phone face-down on the rubber mat. Her hands are stinging with phantom guilt, but she snaps on a fresh pair of purple nitrile gloves and picks up the micropipette. This lab is her clean room. She won't let the mess ruin her standard curve.
        System: [STAT_UPDATE: +Self-Respect, -Emotional Labor]

Narrator: [HIDE: all]
Narrator: [BG: Life_Sciences_Corridor.jpg] [BACKGROUND: Life Sciences basement corridor, 5:50 p.m. Dim fluorescent strips flicker overhead. The air smells sharply of 70% ethanol, autoclave steam, and cold waxed linoleum.]
Narrator: [AUDIO: The low, rhythmic shudder of industrial air scrubbers and the sudden clatter of plastic tubes on tile.]
Narrator: An hour later, at 5:50 p.m., after locking her fluorescence data into the lab server, Nora packs her backpack, pulls up the hood of her charcoal biotechnology sweatshirt, and badges out through the heavy positive-pressure airlock doors into the Life Sciences basement corridor.
Narrator: Her mind is still heavy with the lingering static of Camille's messages as she rounds the blind corner by the stairwell -- and collides squarely with a girl hurrying the opposite way with her arms full of crushed ice.
Maya_Distressed(left): [SPRITE: Nora_Calm, right] Sorry, sorry, I wasn't looking! Don't roll into the grate, please don't roll into the grate--
Narrator: [SPRITE: Jules_Tired, middle] The blue styrofoam ice bucket tips violently sideways. The girl gasps, pinning two escaping microcentrifuge tubes against her ribs with her forearm while two more skitter across the freshly waxed linoleum directly toward a brass floor drain.
Narrator: Through the wire-mesh glass of the heavy stairwell fire door, a guy in a dark jacket with a laptop bag digging a deep groove into his shoulder pauses for a second, glancing back at the commotion with hollow, exhausted eyes before heading out into the evening wind.
Narrator: [HIDE: middle] Nora's lab reflexes take over before she even thinks. She drops smoothly to one knee on the cold tile, intercepting both runaway vials an inch from the drain grate, checking their parafilm seals with a practiced thumb, and steadying the girl's tilting styrofoam bucket.
Nora_Calm(right): No, that was 100% me. I came through the airlock door too fast without checking the convex mirror. Here -- parafilm's still tight, no cracks in the polypropylene. You're doing cortisol assays?
Narrator: The girl blinks rapidly, her chest heaving as she tucks the rescued tubes back into the ice. She has dark hair pulled back in a failing knot held by a #2 pencil, mud-scuffed barn boots, deeply tired eyes, and a lanyard that reads *MAYA - ANIMAL SCI*.
Maya_Speaking(left): Yeah. Bovine serum cortisol. How on earth did you know that from looking at the floor?
Nora_Speaking(right): The blue o-ring screw-caps with the white labeling gaskets. That's the Cayman Chemical competitive ELISA panel, right? My PI used to run the exact same kit on mouse serum before we moved fully into CRISPR-Cas12 diagnostics. Wait -- are you walking toward the core facility plate reader down this hall?
Maya_Tired(left): (groaning softly, resting her forehead against the top of her ice bucket) 'Walking toward' and 'praying to' are the operative words. The Windows XP software on the Life Sciences unit froze three times before lunch today. I think that machine hates me specifically.
Nora_Smiling(right): (a real, dry smirk tugging at her mouth) That BioTek reader was built during the Pedro Rosselló administration and hasn't seen a calibration tech since 2014. Come on. Our suite's absorbance reader upstairs in Biotech 200 gets optical-calibrated every Monday morning. I'll badge you in and set up your template.
Narrator: [SPRITE: Maya_Smiling, left] Maya stares at her for a second like she might actually cry from sheer relief.
Maya_Smiling(left): Are you serious? You're an actual lifesaver. Like, I will name my firstborn calf after you. I'm Maya, by the way. Animal Science. I owe you a coffee the size of your head.
Nora_Speaking_Hoodie(right): Anytime. I'm two benches over from the elevators. Nora.
Narrator: [SPRITE: Nora_Calm, right] As they turn and walk toward the freight elevator, Nora's phone buzzes sharply inside her hoodie pocket -- two short vibrations, then a long one. Another follow-up text from Camille.
Narrator: Nora doesn't pull it out, but her jaw tightens automatically on a three-count inhale. Beside her, Maya stops talking about cattle and glances sideways at Nora's face with startling, quiet perception.
Maya_Thinking(left): Hey. Everything okay? If you have somewhere you need to be at six, I don't want to hold you up--
Nora_Calm(right): No, I've got time. It's just campus noise. Let's get your samples on the scanner before your ice melts.
Narrator: [BG: Biotech_Lab.jpg] They talk the whole forty minutes it takes to run the 96-well plates upstairs in Biotech 200 -- about stress hormones, reproductive genetics, and the lonely 4:30 a.m. grind of the dairy barn. When Nora finally walks home to her apartment in the dark at seven o'clock, she realizes with a strange, quiet jolt that a stranger holding a bucket of cow plasma in a basement hallway was the first person in three weeks to look at her face and ask if she was okay.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scene 2: Tuesday - The Metronome & The Grind
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Narrator: [Scene 2: Tuesday - The Metronome & The Grind] [HIDE: all]
Narrator: [BG: Biotech_Lab.jpg] [BACKGROUND: The shared Molecular Biotechnology laboratory (Biotech 200), Tuesday, 2:15 p.m. Golden afternoon sunlight angles across black epoxy countertops and racks of scintillation vials.]
Narrator: [AUDIO: The soft, steady hum of the air scrubbers and the periodic whirr of a benchtop vortex mixer.]
Narrator: [SPRITE: Maya_Smiling, left] [SPRITE: Nora_Calm, right] Maya makes good on her promise early Tuesday afternoon. At 2:15 p.m., she badges through the hallway doors and slides onto a high steel swivel stool at the end of Nora's bench, setting down a steaming paper cup of dark roast from *The Mill* and an ice-cold can of white Monster Energy with a conspiratorial grin.
Maya_Smiling(left): Payment for services rendered. One caffeinated lifeline, two options depending on whether your afternoon requires warm comfort or synthetic cardiac arrest.
Nora_Smiling(right): (setting down her stylus and cracking open the tab on the Monster can with a crisp hiss) You didn't have to bring a bribe, Sterling. How did the ELISA curves look?
Maya_Speaking(left): Are you kidding? 0.998 R-squared across all forty-eight wells! Dr. Vasquez actually smiled for 1.5 seconds this morning. If you hadn't badged me into your suite last night, Dr. Hale would already be sharpening his knife for my fellowship interview on Friday.
Narrator: [SPRITE: Nora_Laughing, right] Nora takes a massive swig of Monster, does a goofy little celebratory wiggle on her steel swivel stool, and immediately grabs a roll of rainbow lab tape and a pink Sharpie from her bench caddy.
Nora_Speaking(right): As Chief Plate-Reader Sovereign, I hereby bestow upon you the highest honor in biotechnology.
Narrator: [SPRITE: Nora_MonsterEnergy, right] With childish glee, Nora doodles a round, lumpy cartoon cow with stick legs, giant bug eyes, and two crooked horns on the tape, peels it off, and slaps it directly onto Maya's paper coffee cup.
Maya_Thinking(left): (deadpan, tilting her head at the sticker) Vance, you are a Callahan Prize finalist and you just defaced my dark roast with a mutant baked potato that has horns.
Nora_Smiling(right): It's a prize Holstein, Sterling! Her name is Beatrice and she believes in your cortisol levels. Do not insult her artistic pedigree.
Narrator: For the next twenty minutes, Maya perches comfortably on the stool while Nora cleans up vector graphics and receiver-operating characteristic curves on her laptop for Thursday's symposium poster.
Narrator: It is nice just having someone sit at the end of her bench who doesn't want anything from her. Yet after a few minutes of quiet companionship, Maya tilts her head, studying Nora carefully.
Maya_Thinking(left): You know, watching you work is kind of hypnotic. And a little eerie. You sigh like a metronome, Vance. Tick, tick, exhale. Every sixty seconds, right on schedule. How are you like this?
Nora_Thinking(right): (glancing up from her GraphPad Prism window) Like what?
Maya_Speaking(left): So completely unshakeable. You're up for the Callahan Prize in less than forty-eight hours in front of the whole college, and your heart rate looks like it hasn't broken fifty-five all semester.
Narrator: [SPRITE: Nora_Tired, right] Nora pauses, her fingers resting on the cool aluminum of her laptop. For a second, the effortless lab-veteran mask slips -- not into panic, but into the quiet, bone-deep exhaustion of someone who has been carrying two other people's emotional luggage up a mountain without a break.
Nora_Tired(right): It's not calm, Maya. I compartmentalize.
Nora_Speaking(right): In here, if you follow the protocol and keep your bench clean, the assay works. It's fair. Outside these glass doors... my girlfriend is four hundred miles away at grad school having three crises a day that I'm expected to fix over the phone, and my family still doesn't remember what my major is called because they're too busy throwing parades for my brother's finance interviews.
Nora_Tired(right): If I don't build an airlock at the door of this lab, the water gets in. And if the water gets in, I drown.
Narrator: Maya doesn't offer a cheap platitude or change the subject. Her gaze softens with immediate, grounded understanding.
Maya_Smiling(left): Well. If the airlock ever springs a leak this week, you've got permanent guest clearance at the dairy barn. Cows weigh twelve hundred pounds, they radiate at a hundred and one degrees like giant space heaters, and they never send passive-aggressive texts.
Nora_Smiling(right): (a real, quiet laugh warming her chest) That sounds like actual heaven, Sterling.
Narrator: [SPRITE: Maya_Stressed, left] A few minutes later, while Nora steps over to the micropipette rack to grab a box of filter tips, she catches Maya leaning against the brushed steel of the centrifuge bay, staring fixedly at a transcript PDF on her phone screen -- specifically at a sophomore-year `ANSC 101 - D-` sandwiched between columns of freshman and upper-division straight A's.
Nora_Thinking(right): If you stare at that PDF any harder, Sterling, you're going to induce point mutations in the data through sheer force of anxiety.
Maya_Stressed(left): (jumping slightly, shoving the phone into her pocket) I wasn't staring. I was reviewing.
Nora_Calm(right): Right. Reviewing. Like I'm just 'reviewing' this restriction enzyme. I saw the V-shaped dip in the middle of the page, Maya. Straight A's, one sophomore stumble with Hale, and straight A's ever since.
Narrator: [SPRITE: Nora_Distressed, right] Right as Nora opens her mouth to tell her that institutional validation is a trap, Nora's own phone buzzes violently on the epoxy counter:\n*Camille <3: Are we actually going to talk tonight or are you hiding in the lab again? My advisor literally tore my outline apart.*
Narrator: [SPRITE: Nora_Calm, right] Nora's jaw tightens. Without breaking rhythm, she reaches out, flips the phone face-down on the mat, and looks Maya dead in the eye to give her the exact truth she wishes someone would say to her:
Nora_Speaking(right): Listen to me, Maya. Do you know what scientists call an experiment that goes smoothly on the first try, crashes on the second, and gets rebuilt at 99% efficiency on the third? Reproducible validation. Impostor syndrome implies you tricked someone to get here. You didn't trick forty cows into getting pregnant. You did the work after you fell. Hale is just a guy in a tweed jacket who hasn't touched manure in twenty years. Don't let an old memory evict you from a house you built.
Maya_Smiling(left): (visibly steadied, her shoulders dropping an inch) Thanks, Nora. Seriously. And hey... are you okay? With Camille?
Nora_Tired(right): (giving a small, hollow chuckle) Ask me again after Thursday at four. Let's just both survive our panels first.
Narrator: [HIDE: left] When Maya heads back to the agricultural wing, Nora spends the rest of Tuesday afternoon finalizing her 36x48-inch symposium poster layout with Dr. Okafor.
Narrator: [BG: Ag_Sci_Hallway.jpg] [HIDE: all] [SPRITE: Nora_Tired, right] She exports the high-resolution PDF at 4:40 p.m. and walks briskly across campus to the Student Print Center to run her full-color proof before the 5:00 p.m. cutoff.
Narrator: At 4:58 p.m., halfway through printing someone's architecture banner, the print shop's large-format HP DesignJet plotter makes a horrific grinding noise, shreds three feet of glossy paper, and flashes `ERROR 79: TURN OFF THEN ON`. The undergraduate desk clerk looks at the clock, shrugs unapologetically, hangs an *OUT OF ORDER* sign on the glass, and locks the door for the night.
Nora_Tired(right): (leaning her forehead against the cool hallway drywall, sighing to herself) Of course. Because why would a single piece of hardware on this campus cooperate before Thursday?
Narrator: [SPRITE: Jules_Stressed, left] Stepping out into the print center alcove at 5:05 p.m. with her unprinted poster drive still in her pocket, Nora passes that same dark-haired guy from Monday's basement fire door -- Julian "Jules" Rivera.
Narrator: He is standing in front of the glowing beverage vending machine, pulling his third canned espresso of the afternoon from the dispenser slot while staring down at a wall of C++ compiler warnings on his phone screen like a man preparing for a medieval siege.
Narrator: He glances up, recognizes her from the basement corridor, and offers a brief, wordless, deeply exhausted nod of mutual solidarity. Nora nods back.
Narrator: [BG: Noras_Bedroom.jpg] [HIDE: all] [SPRITE: Nora_Hoodie, middle] With the print center locked down, there is only one machine left on the entire campus that can print 11x17 heavyweight tiling proofs after hours without a faculty override code: the ancient, bulletproof Lexmark laser printer tucked in the back corner of *The Mill*.
Narrator: After enduring a tense, forty-minute phone call with Camille at nine o'clock in her bedroom -- where Camille cried about her thesis outline, complained that Nora "sounded tired and unsupportive," and hung up without asking a single question about the jammed poster -- Nora waits until the midnight cafe rush dies down, packs her tiling PDFs onto a flash drive, and walks through the mist to *The Mill*.

Narrator: [BG: The_Mill_Night.jpg] [HIDE: all] [BACKGROUND: The Mill, campus 24-hour cafe, 1:45 a.m. Wednesday. The amber neon sign buzzes softly against the rain-streaked window. The laminate tables are sticky, and the air smells of burnt dark roast and stale adrenaline.]
Narrator: [AUDIO: The rhythmic, mechanical clunk and whirr of the ancient laser printer churning in the back corner.]
Narrator: [SPRITE: Maya_Tired, left] [SPRITE: Nora_Calm, right] At 1:45 a.m. Wednesday, Nora pushes open the heavy glass door of the cafe in oversized gray sweatpants, hauling a three-foot cardboard tube of draft proofs under her arm, completely unbothered by the hour.
Narrator: Just inside the doorway, she nearly bumps into Maya Sterling, who is zipping her battered laptop into her backpack after wrestling her Horizon Fellowship personal statement down to 750 words even.
Maya_Smiling(left): (offering a tired two-finger salute as they pass) Morning shift's all yours, Vance. Careful in the corner booth -- he bites about adjectives and CMake.
Nora_Speaking_Hoodie(right): Noted. Go get some sleep before the cows wake up, Sterling.
Narrator: [HIDE: left] [SPRITE: Jules_Tired, left] Nora plugs her USB drive into the back-corner laser printer, starts her twelve-page tiling job, and glances over at the corner booth Maya just warned her about.
Narrator: Julian "Jules" Rivera is folded into the vinyl bench behind a full-scale military fortification: three portable monitors fanned out in a defensive perimeter, an external mechanical keyboard, four empty espresso cups, and an Adidas shoebox overflowing with color-coded 3x5 flashcards. He has a yellow highlighter tucked behind his left ear and a red Sharpie behind his right like insect antennae.
Nora_Speaking_Hoodie(right): (reaching out with one finger to tap the yellow highlighter perched over his ear with a soft 'boop') Command bunker online. What's the status, Dry Bones Deku? Is this a study system, a server farm, or a hostage situation?
Jules_Smiling(left): (a real, tired laugh escaping him as his hand flies up to swat playfully at the highlighter) Little of all three. Mostly a hostage situation where I'm both the negotiator and the guy tied to the chair. Casualties are high, Lieutenant. My brain cells have filed for collective bargaining, and my terminal has been frozen at 94% since August 9, 1945.
Nora_Laughing(right): (snickering, leaning on the back of the empty chair) Have you tried using your sweet voice impressions? Like, 'Pretty please, Mr. Compiler, don't crash or Julian will have to eat dirt'?
Jules_Tired(left): I offered it my left kidney and a signed confession to an unsolved jewel heist. It countered with fourteen deprecation warnings.
Nora_Laughing(right): (sticking her tongue out playfully) Bleh. Deprecation warnings are just computer gossip. It's like, 'Ooh, look at him using an old pointer, how scandalous!' Just hit it with a hammer. That's what we do in biotech when the centrifuge makes scary noises.
Jules_Tired(left): I've rewritten my capstone discussion section six times since Sunday. I'm starting to worry the seventh time is just going to be the first time with worse grammar and more sleep-deprived typos.
Narrator: Nora perches effortlessly on the wooden arm of the chair across from him, cradling a paper cup of hot chamomile tea between her palms, finding a strange comfort in talking to someone who is just as awake at 2:00 a.m. as she is, but who isn't asking her to fix his life.
Nora_Speaking_Hoodie(right): Six rewrites. Okay. You're Julian, right? From the print shop vending machine at five?
Jules_Speaking(left): Jules. Yeah. And you're Nora. Did the print center ever un-jam your poster?
Nora_Calm(right): The student clerk declared it a federal disaster area at 5:02 and locked the door in my face. Hence why I'm bribing The Mill's dinosaur laser printer to spit out my 11x17 tiling proofs. Can I ask you a nosy question, Jules?
Jules_Tired(left): You're printing molecular biology proofs at two in the morning in a coffee shop, Nora. I feel like we're well past the social contract of asking permission. Shoot.
Nora_Speaking_Hoodie(right): When I saw you by the vending machine at five, and when I look at this booth right now... does it ever feel like the whole thing -- the GPA, the capstone, the corporate interview, all of it -- is just one long held breath? Like the entire building is balanced on your collarbone and something's about to shatter if you exhale wrong?
Jules_Distressed(left): (his voice dropping quiet in the hum of the cafe) I... yeah. Every single day since August. Actually, every semester since freshman year when my dad framed my first Dean's List letter next to the front door.
Narrator: [Nora looking at him steadily -- not judging it, not flinching from it, recognizing a different dialect of the exact same pressure she carries for her family]
Nora_Calm(right): My sepsis biosensor project -- the one in that printer tray right now -- has failed eleven times since March. Eleven. Complete, catastrophic assay failures. I have a whole Excel spreadsheet dedicated entirely to tracking the wreckage. Dr. Okafor calls it the Graveyard Tab.
Nora_Speaking_Hoodie(right): And honestly? It's the most interesting thing I've worked on in four years, specifically because it keeps failing in brand-new, spectacularly annoying ways. Every time a prototype dies, it tells me where the chemistry is actually happening.
Jules_Stressed(left): That's not the same as maintaining a 3.9 GPA for an aerospace recruiter, Nora. Engineering doesn't celebrate failure as an aesthetic.
Nora_Speaking_Hoodie(right): No. It's not. I'm not saying trade your major in or go tank your capstone tomorrow. I'm just asking a smaller question: is there one class, just one, this semester, where you let yourself be bad at something on purpose? Not reckless-bad. Just human-bad. Unoptimized. See what's on the other side of that glass.

? Narrator: How does Nora respond when Jules pushes back with his suffocating academic terror?
    > "It's just one project, Jules. Try doing it because you like it, not out of fear." (Challenge the perfectionism)
        Jules_Distressed(left): Do you really not feel behind, ever? Like if you drop one ball, the people counting on you are going to look at you and realize you were nothing special all along?
        Narrator: The question hits Nora right where her own mother and Camille live, but looking at Jules's hollow eyes, she refuses to hand him more armor.
        Nora_Calm(right): Sometimes. Every time my phone rings after 8:00 p.m., actually. But that's about the stuff outside the lab.
        Nora_Speaking_Hoodie(right): The work itself -- the science -- shouldn't feel like a hostage situation you have to survive just so people will love you, Jules. I actually like doing my work because in the lab, it's mine. Maybe try picking just one class or one afternoon where you don't need to be a flawless machine. See what's left of Julian when the fear isn't doing the driving.
        Narrator: [SPRITE: Jules_Thinking, left] Jules stares at her for a long, quiet beat. Slowly, his rigid shoulders drop a fraction of an inch. He reaches into his Adidas shoebox, pulls out a blank white index card with no color code, and writes six deliberate words across the center: *one class, not perfect, see what happens.*
        Narrator: [SPRITE: Nora_Laughing, right] Nora watches him write the words. Then, without asking, she pulls a glittery lilac gel pen with a wobbly silicone dinosaur topper from her sweatpants pocket, leans across the table, and doodles a tiny, derpy potato with big ears and a goofy smile right in the bottom corner of the card.
        Jules_Thinking(left): (blinking down at the ink) Did you just draw a deformed potato on my existential manifesto?
        Nora_Speaking_Hoodie(right): It's a derpy potato, Rivera. He's your emotional support tuber now. You legally cannot break a contract when a potato with ears is supervising you.
        Jules_Smiling(left): (a quiet, genuine laugh slipping through his exhaustion as he shakes his head) An emotional support tuber. Dr. Okoye is going to revoke my degree and my student rights.
        Narrator: He stares at the ink for a beat. He doesn't throw the card away, and he doesn't file it into the shoebox; he tucks it into the breast pocket of his jacket, right over his ribs.
        Nora_Calm(right): (watching him with a warm, approving nod) That's a clean baseline, Rivera.
        System: [STAT_UPDATE: +Chosen Family, +Self-Respect]
    > "I get it. The pressure is insane. You just have to survive it." (Validate the survival mode)
        Jules_Distressed(left): Do you not feel behind, ever? Like something's about to fall apart if you stop moving for five minutes?
        Nora_Hoodie(right): (looking down into her lukewarm paper cup, her own exhaustion catching up with her at two in the morning) Of course I do, Jules. The pressure in this place -- and from the people waiting at home -- is insane. Honestly, sometimes you just have to seal the airlock, put your head down, and survive the week however you can. That's all I'm doing right now.
        Jules_Stressed(left): Exactly. Sympathy and rest are luxuries we can't afford before Friday. Just keep swimming or drown.
        Narrator: They sit in a heavy, quiet truce across the espresso-stained laminate -- two exhausted seniors locked inside their own separate survival bunkers while the rain taps against the glass.
        System: [STAT_UPDATE: -Energy]

Narrator: In the back corner, the Lexmark printer drops the final 11x17 proof sheet into the tray. Nora stands up, gathers the warm pages, and slides them carefully into her cardboard tube.
Narrator: Before heading out into the mist, she pauses beside Jules's monitor wall, suddenly realizing she doesn't want to stand in front of that symposium podium on Thursday with only empty guest chairs looking back at her.
Nora_Calm(right): Hey, Jules. The Undergraduate Research Symposium is Thursday afternoon, by the way. Two-thirty, CID Building Atrium, Slot Two. No pressure at all -- I know Thursday is your big deadline.
Nora_Speaking_Hoodie(right): It's just... my family isn't going to make it, and my girlfriend's four hundred miles away. It would be nice if a friendly face was out there that wasn't my PI staring through my soul.
Jules_Speaking(left): Thursday at two-thirty. CID Atrium. I'll put it on the whiteboard, Nora.
Nora_Calm(right): Don't put it in red marker. Red looks too much like a compiler error. Goodnight, Jules.

