Narrator: [SPRITE: Nora_Distressed, middle] Nora stares at the two texts on her screen, then up at Maya's $4.99 carnations and Jules's outstretched phone.
Narrator: For one desperate heartbeat, she wants to say yes. She wants to throw her phone into a biohazard bin, walk out of this atrium with the two people who actually sat in the third row for her, and eat hot food under the patio lights.
Narrator: But twenty-one years of conditioning is a heavy chain. The familiar, suffocating guilt of being the "responsible one" wraps tight around her ribs. If Camille is crying in her car right now and Nora disappears for two hours to get pizza, it will turn into a three-day war. If she doesn't call her mother back to gush over Daniel's job offer before they leave for Morton's, her mother will call her cold and selfish.
Narrator: Nora swallows hard, forcing her face back into the smooth, polite mask of the girl who never needs anything.
Nora_Tired(middle): I... I really want to, guys, but I should stay here and make a few calls. Camille is having a massive breakdown over her thesis advisor in her car right now, I really need to talk her down. And I have to call my mom back to congratulate my brother on his job offer before they go to dinner.
Narrator: [SPRITE: Jules_Tired, left] [SPRITE: Maya_Distressed, right] Maya's bright grin falters, the cellophane-wrapped carnations lowering an inch in her hands. Beside her, Jules frowns quietly, locking his phone screen and slipping the video of her triumph back into his pocket.
Jules_Stressed(left): Nora... you just won Second Place out of forty projects in the entire college. Can't their stuff wait one hour so you can celebrate?
Nora_Distressed(middle): (her voice cracking slightly beneath the polite veneer) I know. It's fine, Jules, really. You guys have no idea how much it means to me that you came today, I just... I have to handle this first, okay? Rain check?
Maya_Tired(right): (studying Nora's face with a deep, sad, quiet understanding, then gently pressing the gas-station carnations into Nora's crook of her arm anyway) ...Sure, Vance. Rain check. Take the flowers, at least. Don't let them convince you today wasn't a big deal.
Narrator: [HIDE: left] [HIDE: right] Maya gives her shoulder a gentle squeeze, and she and Jules turn and walk out through the glass double doors into the clearing afternoon light.
Narrator: Nora stands completely alone beside Board #2 as the janitorial crew begins folding up the empty chairs around her. Taking a sixty-second metronome breath to steady her voice, she dials Camille's number, bracing herself to spend the evening of her greatest academic triumph apologizing to someone who forgot it happened.
System: [STAT_UPDATE: Emotional Labor MAXED, Self-Respect DEPLETED]

