Narrator: [BG: Noras_Bedroom.jpg] [BACKGROUND: Nora's sparse studio bedroom, Friday morning, 10:15 a.m. The blinds are half-drawn, keeping the room in a flat, gray shadow. On the wall, the taped-up mock-up poster has peeled loose at one top corner, hanging crookedly.]
Narrator: [AUDIO: The oppressive, mechanical ticking of a wall clock, layered over a low, melancholy ambient drone.]
Narrator: [SPRITE: Nora_Hoodie, middle] Nora sits on the edge of her unmade bed in yesterday's rumpled sweatpants, her shoulder aching from holding her warm cell phone pressed to her ear for the last seventy-five minutes.
Narrator: Beside her on the nightstand, the Shell gas-station carnations Maya bought her are still wrapped in their crinkly plastic -- Nora was on the phone with her mother until ten last night praising Daniel's signing bonus and listening to a play-by-play of the steakhouse appetizers, and then on the phone with Camille until 1:30 a.m., so she never even got around to putting them in water.
Camille: (through the speaker, her voice animated and gossipy again now that Nora has absorbed all her stress) ...and then after all that drama yesterday, my roommate had the absolute nerve this morning to ask if she could borrow my printer paper for her seminar! Can you believe the audacity?
Nora_Speaking_Hoodie(middle): (her voice hollow, quiet, and automatic) Yeah, Cam. That's crazy.
Camille: Right?! Anyway, thank god you talked me off the ledge last night, babe, I don't know what I'd do without you, you're literally my rock. Oh, wait -- hold on, Nora, I have to go, my cohort friend just pulled up outside to take me to brunch! We'll do a FaceTime date this weekend and you can tell me all about your little poster thing, okay? Love you, bye!
Narrator: [SPRITE: Nora_Distressed, middle] The line clicks dead.
Narrator: Slowly, Nora lowers the phone from her ear. Her arm feels like it weighs a hundred pounds.
Narrator: She looks across the small room at her desk. The blue-and-silver satin Second Place Callahan Prize ribbon is lying flat on the wood, already half-buried under a stack of printed journal articles Dr. Okafor emailed her at dawn for next month's manuscript revisions.
Narrator: She had done everything she was supposed to do. She had kept the peace. She had been the supportive girlfriend and the easygoing, low-maintenance daughter. She had protected everyone else's feelings from the inconvenience of her own needs.
Narrator: And sitting here in the dead silence of her apartment while Camille goes to brunch and her family celebrates Daniel, Nora finally realizes the truth: she isn't in a relationship with them anymore. She is just the only one left in the room keeping the machines on life support.
Narrator: On the mattress beside her knee, her phone screen lights up with a quiet text notification from Maya:\n*Maya: Hey Vance. Just walked out of my Horizon panel with Hale -- survived!! Jules and I are grabbing coffee and pancakes at The Mill patio right now. The metronome still ticking? Come join us if you're free.*
Narrator: Nora stares at the glowing message for a long time.
Narrator: She picks up the phone. Her thumbs type out: *I'd love to, be there in ten.*
Narrator: Then she looks at the unwashed coffee mugs in her sink, the crooked poster on her wall, and the sheer, crushing exhaustion in her bones from giving away every drop of energy she had left to people who didn't even notice. She feels too hollowed out to be around human beings today -- even the ones who care.
Narrator: Slowly, Nora holds down the backspace key until the text box is blank. She flips the phone face-down on the mattress, stands up, and picks up a yellow highlighter and a printed journal article on Cas12a kinetics.
Narrator: [HIDE: all] She walks back to her desk and sits down alone in the gray light. The airlock doors hiss shut. Inside, the stoichiometry is clean, nobody can hurt her, and she is entirely, achingly alone.
System: *Ending B: The Echo Chamber*